      "observed": "The schematic annotation says to short R13 and R14 for serial servos, but R13 is 2.2 kΩ in the MAX3485 line network and R14 is 20 kΩ in the Q3 control network. The 0 Ω parts shown in the servo signal circuitry are R12 and R20, so following the annotation does not make the documented serial-mode connections.",
      "expected": "The serial-mode assembly instruction should identify the actual links that connect J4 and J11 signal pins to the shared MAX3485 servo bus, as specified for R13 and R14 in the design specification.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Short R13, R14 for Serial Servo; R13 2.2k; R14 20k; R12 0; R20 0 (sheet /, page 1)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "R13, R14 | 0 Ω (jumpers) | When shorted, bridge J3-pin3 and J8-pin3 together and onto the MAX3485 TTL bus (page 1)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "Serial mode (populate R13/R14, per the explicit schematic annotation “Short R13, R14 for Serial Servo”) (page 1)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "An assembler following the documented serial-mode instruction cannot make the specified signal connections using R13 and R14; those parts belong to other circuit functions. The board may therefore fail to operate with serial servos, or the control/bias circuitry may be altered if those resistors are modified as though they were jumpers.",
      "confidence": 0.88
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic’s serial-servo jumper instruction conflicts with the actual component identities and values: R13 and R14 are not 0 Ω signal links. This makes the documented serial-mode assembly configuration unreliable."
}

